true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Reality",": "]}],"Engineers talk in OSI layer numbers all the time (\"a layer-4 load balancer\", \"a layer-7 proxy\") even though the internet runs on TCP/IP."]}]]}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L24",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/cn-notes/objects/ch10%2F03-osi-7.jpg?v=4a96d1612e05e9cd6b2b431818080b5d","alt":"The seven OSI layers numbered 7 down to 1."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]]}]]}]
29:["$","section","04-osi-vs-tcpip",{"id":"04-osi-vs-tcpip","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"04"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"How they map together"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The two models are not rivals, they line up. OSI’s top three (Application, Presentation, Session) collapse into TCP/IP’s Application. OSI Transport = TCP/IP Transport, OSI Network = TCP/IP Internet, and OSI’s bottom two (Data Link, Physical) become TCP/IP’s Link."}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L24",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/cn-notes/objects/ch10%2F04-osi-vs-tcpip.jpg?v=2a856243ecd7636cc18d285aa1508850","alt":"Arrows mapping OSI 7/6/5 to Application, 4 to Transport, 3 to Internet, 2/1 to Link."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]]}]]}]
2a:["$","section","05-encapsulation",{"id":"05-encapsulation","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"05"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"A header per layer (encapsulation)"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Layers are why encapsulation (Chapter 1) works the way it does. As data goes down the stack, each layer wraps it with its own header: Transport adds the TCP/UDP header (→ segment/datagram), Internet adds the IP header (→ packet), Link adds the frame header (→ frame). At the receiver it is unwrapped in reverse."}],["$","div","1",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Symmetry",": "]}],"Down the stack = wrap. Up the stack = unwrap. Each layer only reads its own header."]}]]}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L24",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/cn-notes/objects/ch10%2F05-encapsulation.jpg?v=e731d66143945aaea659ec4c6e47f93d","alt":"Data becomes Segment (+TCP), then Packet (+IP), then Frame (+frame header), then bits."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]]}]]}]
2b:["$","section","06-mnemonic",{"id":"06-mnemonic","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"06"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Remembering the 7 layers"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"A classic mnemonic for OSI bottom-to-top (Physical → Application): \"Please Do Not Throw Sausage Pizza Away\": Physical, Data link, Network, Transport, Session, Presentation, Application."}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L24",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/cn-notes/objects/ch10%2F06-mnemonic.jpg?v=b9367069d2702fe7230e15034d4e944a","alt":"The mnemonic Please Do Not Throw Sausage Pizza Away mapped to the layers."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]]}]]}]
